<section class="docs-section docs-page">
  <p class="docs-eyebrow">Inertia X Primitive</p>
  <h2>Frame</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;Frame&gt;</code> creates an isolated Inertia region with its own visit lifecycle.
    It is the foundation that powers advanced UI patterns like modals, side panels, and split views.
  </p>

### Basic Usage

```svelte
<script>
  import { Frame } from 'inertiax-svelte'
</script>

<Frame id="settings-panel" src="/settings.json">
  <p>Loading settings...</p>
</Frame>
```

### visitOptions

Use `visitOptions` to set frame-wide navigation defaults.

```svelte
<Frame
  id="sidebar"
  src="/users/42/edit"
  visitOptions={{
    replace: true,
    updateBrowserUrl: false,
    preserveScroll: true,
  }}
/>
```

Top frame defaults are optimized for full-page navigation, while non-top frames default to
`replace: true` and `updateBrowserUrl: false`.

### forceRequest

By default, a frame can restore from history state on remount. Set `forceRequest` when you always
want a fresh request on mount.

```svelte
<!-- Restore previous frame page when possible -->
<Frame id="activity" src="/activity" />

<!-- Always fetch fresh content on mount -->
<Frame id="activity" src="/activity" forceRequest />
```

### TLDR

- Each frame has independent navigation and state.
- Anchor links inside a frame are intercepted automatically.
- Frame state can be restored with browser history.

